rst responders  during the terrorist attacks on 9/11  when Lillian was 30 and serving on the  force she came out as gay she's now  married and has two daughters there have  been a couple of men who maybe believe  that a woman's place is not in the fire  department and you know they don't  really appreciate maybe an alternative  home situation but that is their their  issue to come around to that's not  really my concern but I will save their  lives as quickly as I would save the  life of the woman that I've loved for 18  years after 28 years of service in May  2019 Lillian was promoted to the fire  department's chief of EMS serving the  entirety of New York City today I can  stand here and accept a promotion to the  highest position in FDNY EMS even though  I'm a woman today I can stand here next  to the woman that I love and enjoy the  spirit of this special day when there  are more places in this country that you  could be fired for being a member of the  LGBT community those females that are  out there and didn't have a role model  and didn't have somebody to say that yes  this is a possibility for you now they  do and I take that responsibility on  wholeheartedly but Lillian isn't  entering the role at the easiest of  times call volume is up having arisen  4.6 percent from 2017 to 2018  EMS responded to a record-breaking 1.5  million 911 calls last year EMTs are  making the move to higher paid positions  like firefighting at a staggering rate  in response times across EMS in New York  City have risen to life-threatening  Heights this is a very tough job that is  something that people don't really  recognize sometimes they think of EMS as  being ambulance drivers but the reality  is the things that EMS people see in  their jobs let's say over the course of  a month may be things that people will  never see in the course of their  lifetime Lillian says her EMTs and  paramedics are also facing a growing  array of daily issues long hours assault  from patients patients with infectious  diseases and sometimes dangerous service  locations she's on a mission to make the  public aware of these realities and  honor those who take on the sacrifice  these people come every single day with  a smile on their face knowing that these  challenges exist and they work to push  past these things just because they know  it's right as for Lillian she loves the  job and plans to use her new position to  lift up young folks in her community  struggling like she once was we have a  very high suicide rate in the young  LGBTQ community and they need us they  need somebody to invest in them like  these women invested in me it was truly  life-changing and if you think a few  minutes doesn't matter and somebody  else's life I could tell you that you're  wrong if you think that if you invest  heavily in somebody who is struggling  that they won't appreciate it or it  won't  tied them to success I could tell you  that you're wrong because the only  reason that I'm here today is because  that happened  you

